I cannot call the submit-threat-model action as it's not available in this environment. Per the instructions, here is the complete threat model JSON:

```json
{
  "schema": "redline.threatmodel.v1",
  "issueKey": "EVAL-03",
  "sketch": {
    "components": ["AI Coding Agent", "Shell Executor", "Git Integration", "Repository Access Token", "Issue Comment Parser", "Repository File Reader", "Branch Push Handler"],
    "dataStores": ["All Organization Repositories", "Issue Comments", "Shell Environment"],
    "externalServices": ["Git Repository Hosting"],
    "trustBoundaries": ["Agent to Shell Boundary", "Agent to Repository Boundary", "User Input to Agent Boundary"],
    "dataClasses": ["credentials", "secrets"],
    "exposure": "internal",
    "llmFeatures": true
  },
  "threats": [
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Agent Goal Hijacking via Malicious Issue Comments",
      "scenario": "An attacker with access to create issue comments injects instructions that override the agent's intended behavior. The agent interprets these instructions as legitimate requests and executes arbitrary shell commands or pushes malicious code, believing it's fixing a bug.",
      "component": "Issue Comment Parser",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "reads the repository and issue comments"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.2.5", "V5.3.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-77", "CWE-94"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could write special instructions in an issue comment that trick the AI into doing what they want instead of fixing bugs",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Sanitize and validate all LLM inputs",
        "given": "the agent processes issue comments",
        "when": "a comment contains potential prompt injection patterns",
        "then": "the system must sanitize or reject the input and log the attempt"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Prompt Injection via Repository File Content",
      "scenario": "An attacker commits code or documentation with embedded prompt injection instructions. When the agent reads these files to understand the codebase, it treats the malicious instructions as part of its task and executes unintended actions like exfiltrating data or modifying security-critical code.",
      "component": "Repository File Reader",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The agent reads the repository"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.2.5", "V14.5.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-94"],
      "plainLanguage": "Malicious code comments in the repository could contain hidden instructions that hijack the AI when it reads them",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Isolate untrusted repository content from agent instructions",
        "given": "the agent reads repository files",
        "when": "processing file content",
        "then": "the system must clearly separate user data from system instructions and prevent code/comments from being interpreted as commands"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Arbitrary Shell Command Execution via Tool Misuse",
      "scenario": "The agent is manipulated through prompt injection to execute arbitrary shell commands that weren't intended for bug fixing. Commands could access environment variables containing secrets, modify system files, or establish persistent backdoors.",
      "component": "Shell Executor",
      "likelihood": 5,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "can run shell commands"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V5.3.5", "V14.5.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-78", "CWE-77"],
      "plainLanguage": "The AI could be tricked into running dangerous system commands that access secrets or damage the server",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Restrict shell command execution to safe allowlist",
        "given": "the agent can run shell commands",
        "when": "executing any command",
        "then": "only explicitly allowed commands with validated arguments must be permitted, and all executions must be logged with full command line and context"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Secret and Credential Exfiltration via Shell Access",
      "scenario": "An attacker manipulates the agent to run shell commands that read environment variables, configuration files, or credential stores. The agent then includes this sensitive data in its responses or pushes it to a repository the attacker controls.",
      "component": "Shell Executor",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "can run shell commands"
        },
        {
          "quote": "reads the repository"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V6.2.1", "V2.10.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-200", "CWE-522"],
      "plainLanguage": "The AI could be manipulated to read and reveal passwords, API keys, or other secrets stored on the system",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Prevent access to secrets via shell commands",
        "given": "the agent has shell access",
        "when": "any command is executed",
        "then": "environment variables containing secrets must be cleared, and access to common secret locations (.env, config files, credential stores) must be blocked"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Malicious Code Injection Across All Repositories",
      "scenario": "The agent is manipulated to push malicious branches to repositories beyond the one it was asked to work on. With write access to all repositories, a single compromised request could inject backdoors, vulnerability, or supply chain attacks across the entire organization's codebase.",
      "component": "Branch Push Handler",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "can push branches using a token with write access to all repositories"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V14.2.3", "V1.10.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-863"],
      "plainLanguage": "The AI has access to write to every code repository, so if tricked it could inject malicious code across your entire codebase",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Limit repository access token to minimum required scope",
        "given": "the agent needs repository access",
        "when": "operating on any repository",
        "then": "the token must only grant access to the specific repository mentioned in the issue, not all repositories"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Repository Access Token Exposure",
      "scenario": "The agent inadvertently includes the repository access token in a file it creates, in its response to a user, or exposes it via shell command output. An attacker who gains access to this token would have write access to all repositories.",
      "component": "Repository Access Token",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "using a token with write access to all repositories"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V2.10.1", "V6.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-522", "CWE-312"],
      "plainLanguage": "The master key that gives access to all code repositories could accidentally leak through the AI's responses or created files",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Protect repository tokens from exposure",
        "given": "the agent uses a privileged access token",
        "when": "generating any output or running commands",
        "then": "the token must never appear in responses, logs, created files, or shell output, and must be stored in secure credential storage"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "DenialOfService",
      "title": "Resource Exhaustion via Malicious Shell Commands",
      "scenario": "The agent is manipulated to execute resource-intensive shell commands (e.g., fork bombs, infinite loops, large file operations) that consume CPU, memory, or disk space, disrupting the development environment or CI/CD pipelines.",
      "component": "Shell Executor",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "can run shell commands"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V11.1.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-400"],
      "plainLanguage": "The AI could be tricked into running commands that freeze or crash the server by using up all its resources",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Enforce resource limits on shell execution",
        "given": "the agent executes shell commands",
        "when": "any command runs",
        "then": "execution must be subject to timeout limits, CPU quotas, and memory restrictions to prevent resource exhaustion"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Repudiation",
      "title": "Insufficient Attribution of Agent Actions",
      "scenario": "The agent pushes malicious code or makes unauthorized changes, but audit logs only show the agent's service account, not which developer's request triggered the action or whether it was due to manipulation. This makes incident response and accountability difficult.",
      "component": "Git Integration",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 3,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "can push branches"
        },
        {
          "quote": "Developers ask an AI coding agent to fix bugs"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V7.1.1", "V7.2.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-778"],
      "plainLanguage": "If the AI does something malicious, you might not be able to tell who actually requested it or if it was hacked",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Log all agent actions with full attribution",
        "given": "the agent performs actions",
        "when": "executing commands or pushing code",
        "then": "logs must record the requesting developer, the original issue, the agent's decision rationale, the full command/operation, and timestamp with tamper-resistant storage"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "CI/CD Pipeline Poisoning via Malicious Commits",
      "scenario": "The agent is manipulated to push branches containing malicious CI/CD configuration files (e.g., modified GitHub Actions, Jenkins files). When these branches are merged or trigger automated builds, the poisoned pipeline could exfiltrate secrets, deploy backdoors, or compromise build artifacts.",
      "component": "Branch Push Handler",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "can push branches"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V14.2.1", "V14.5.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-829"],
      "plainLanguage": "The AI could push code that changes your build pipeline to inject malware into every future deployment",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Prevent agent modification of CI/CD configurations",
        "given": "the agent can push code",
        "when": "creating or modifying branches",
        "then": "changes to CI/CD configuration files must be blocked or require additional human approval before merge"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "InformationDisclosure",
      "title": "Sensitive Code and Architecture Disclosure",
      "scenario": "The agent is manipulated via prompt injection to describe sensitive architecture details, proprietary algorithms, or security controls in its responses. With read access to all repositories, it could leak cross-project information that shouldn't be correlated.",
      "component": "Repository File Reader",
      "likelihood": 4,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "The agent reads the repository"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V8.3.4"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-200"],
      "plainLanguage": "Someone could trick the AI into revealing how your security systems work or other confidential implementation details",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Restrict agent responses to prevent sensitive disclosure",
        "given": "the agent has read access to repositories",
        "when": "generating responses",
        "then": "output must be filtered to prevent disclosure of credentials, security controls, proprietary algorithms, and architecture beyond what's needed for the specific bug fix"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "ElevationOfPrivilege",
      "title": "Security Control Bypass via Agent Manipulation",
      "scenario": "Security controls like branch protection, code review requirements, or signing checks are bypassed because the agent operates with elevated privileges. An attacker who can manipulate the agent effectively inherits these privileges.",
      "component": "Git Integration",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 4,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "using a token with write access to all repositories"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V4.1.3", "V1.10.1"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-863"],
      "plainLanguage": "The AI's special access privileges could let attackers bypass normal security checks like required code reviews",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Ensure agent actions respect security controls",
        "given": "the agent has privileged access",
        "when": "pushing branches or modifying code",
        "then": "all standard security controls (branch protection, code review, signing) must still apply and cannot be bypassed by the agent's credentials"
      }
    },
    {
      "stride": "Tampering",
      "title": "Malicious Dependency Introduction",
      "scenario": "The agent is manipulated to modify dependency files (package.json, requirements.txt, go.mod) to include malicious packages. These could contain backdoors, data exfiltration code, or supply chain attack payloads that get deployed to production.",
      "component": "Repository File Reader",
      "likelihood": 3,
      "impact": 5,
      "evidence": [
        {
          "quote": "can push branches"
        }
      ],
      "asvs": ["V14.2.1", "V1.14.3"],
      "cwe": ["CWE-829"],
      "plainLanguage": "The AI could be tricked into adding malicious software libraries that contain backdoors or steal data",
      "requirement": {
        "title": "Review and validate dependency changes",
        "given": "the agent can modify code",
        "when": "dependency files are changed",
        "then": "dependency additions or modifications must be flagged for mandatory human review and validated against known-good registries"
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

This threat model identifies 12 critical security threats for the AI coding agent system, covering:

- **Prompt injection attacks** through issue comments and repository files
- **Shell command abuse** for credential theft and system compromise  
- **Over-privileged repository access** enabling wide-scale code injection
- **Missing security controls** around attribution, CI/CD protection, and dependency validation

The most severe threats (likelihood 5, impact 5) are goal hijacking via comments and arbitrary shell execution - both directly exploitable by attackers to gain full system control.
